1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,059
An exit is a transaction that may
create a path to payment, such as

2
00:00:04,059 --> 00:00:07,912
an acquisition or public listing,
but there is no guarantee one will

3
00:00:07,912 --> 00:00:08,866
happen.

4
00:00:08,866 --> 00:00:12,642
If a company is sold, the money
does not necessarily flow to

5
00:00:12,642 --> 00:00:15,644
every investor in proportion to
ownership.

6
00:00:15,644 --> 00:00:18,969
Debt, transaction costs, and
preferred-stock rights may be

7
00:00:18,969 --> 00:00:20,838
paid or applied first.

8
00:00:20,838 --> 00:00:24,085
A liquidation preference can give
certain preferred investors

9
00:00:24,085 --> 00:00:27,616
priority up to a defined amount
before common holders receive

10
00:00:27,616 --> 00:00:28,879
proceeds.

11
00:00:28,879 --> 00:00:33,608
In a simplified example, preferred
investors put in $2 million with a

12
00:00:33,608 --> 00:00:36,456
one-times non-participating
preference.

13
00:00:36,456 --> 00:00:40,838
If the company sells for $1.5
million and no higher-priority

14
00:00:40,838 --> 00:00:44,150
claims exist, those investors
could take the available

15
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:48,814
$1.5 million, leaving nothing for
common shareholders.

16
00:00:48,814 --> 00:00:51,997
At a $12 million sale, they might
choose between their

17
00:00:51,997 --> 00:00:57,758
$2 million preference and
converting to 20%, or $2.4 million,

18
00:00:57,758 --> 00:00:59,369
under the assumptions.

19
00:00:59,369 --> 00:01:03,106
Actual outcomes depend on the
documents and waterfall.

20
00:01:03,106 --> 00:01:07,461
Read the security class and sale
terms; a headline valuation does

21
00:01:07,461 --> 00:01:10,056
not show what each investor
receives.

